WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:05.300
Between the comic book panel and the silver
screen is an often-overlooked conceptual stage

00:00:05.430 --> 00:00:07.160
that's an art form all its own.

00:00:07.460 --> 00:00:12.580
That visual design work is on display in Marvel's
art book Avengers: Infinity War The Art of

00:00:12.580 --> 00:00:13.140
the Movie.

00:00:13.520 --> 00:00:17.880
While we wait and wonder about the fourth
Avengers movie, this new collection provides

00:00:17.880 --> 00:00:21.000
an intriguing look at the Infinity War that
might have been.

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.640
The Avengers are all over the emotional spectrum
when we catch up with them in Infinity War,

00:00:26.640 --> 00:00:28.400
but none are feeling lower than Thor.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.760
His third solo movie, Ragnarok was a cathartic
experience for the character.

00:00:32.770 --> 00:00:37.329
It was a journey that required the God of
Thunder to accept loss and learn to let go

00:00:37.329 --> 00:00:41.400
of his parents, his palace, his eye, and his
beloved hammer, Mjolnir.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:45.400
"it sounds like you had a special and intimate
relationship with this hammer and losing it

00:00:45.400 --> 00:00:47.660
was almost comparable to losing a loved one."

00:00:48.540 --> 00:00:49.640
"that's a nice way of putting it."

00:00:50.220 --> 00:00:54.400
Thor ended the movie growing as a person,
ascending to the throne of Asgard to lead

00:00:54.409 --> 00:00:55.989
his people to a new home.

00:00:55.989 --> 00:01:00.589
Then, before the main title of Infinity War
even appears, Thanos wipes out half of the

00:01:00.589 --> 00:01:01.860
remaining Asgardians.

00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:02.580
Off screen.

00:01:02.860 --> 00:01:07.660
From that point, most of Thor's story involves
forging the axe Stormbreaker to replace his

00:01:07.660 --> 00:01:09.740
long-lost hammer and get some vengeance.

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:14.620
At some point, though, Marvel Studios' head
of visual development Ryan Meinerding imagined

00:01:14.630 --> 00:01:19.119
Thor wielding some sort of laser rifle instead,
which he perhaps would have gotten from his

00:01:19.120 --> 00:01:20.640
new friend Rocket Raccoon.

00:01:20.780 --> 00:01:25.420
It's just as well that Thor never ended up
wielding a gun in Infinity War that's really

00:01:25.420 --> 00:01:26.640
more of a Skurge thing.

00:01:27.860 --> 00:01:33.220
One of Infinity War's bigger surprises was
Eitri, the giant dwarf played by Peter Dinklage.

00:01:33.380 --> 00:01:38.210
The master of the forge of Nidavellir, Eitri
is the last survivor of his people, the rest

00:01:38.210 --> 00:01:42.440
having been wiped out by Thanos after they
created his Infinity Gauntlet.

00:01:42.440 --> 00:01:46.829
With the help of Thor and company, he refires
the forge to make one a new weapon in the

00:01:46.829 --> 00:01:48.740
hopes of thwarting the Mad Titan.

00:01:48.740 --> 00:01:53.720
Of particular help is Groot, who sacrifices
one of his own limbs on the spur of the moment

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.460
to make the mighty axe's handle.

00:01:55.469 --> 00:01:57.659
According to concept artist John Straub,

00:01:57.659 --> 00:02:02.300
"The original scene in the script just said
'Groot helps Eitri,' giving me a lot of freedom

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:04.939
to come up with something interesting and
unique.".

00:02:04.939 --> 00:02:09.410
Before settling on the final version, Straub
imagined a scene in which Groot formed a sort

00:02:09.410 --> 00:02:12.200
of exoskeleton and acted as Eitri's arms.

00:02:12.200 --> 00:02:16.680
Anthony Francisco, meanwhile, offered up a
take on Eitri that was somewhat more aged

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:21.080
and austere than the disheveled survivor Dinklage
became in the finished film.

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.780
The costume Chris Evans wears in Infinity
War is a darker-colored variation on his Captain

00:02:26.780 --> 00:02:30.440
America uniform, with the white star conspicuously
ripped out.

00:02:30.520 --> 00:02:34.960
It's a strong visual indicator that, while
he's still the same selfless Steve Rogers

00:02:34.970 --> 00:02:39.850
we've always known, he doesn't need the Americana
trappings to show what his ideals are.

00:02:39.850 --> 00:02:44.120
It's a great concept but it wasn't the first
concept the film's design team considered.

00:02:44.120 --> 00:02:48.910
For inspiration, the artists also looked toward
the comic book character "U.S. Agent", who

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.900
is typically known for his black-and-red variation
of Cap's classic suit.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:58.000
Before settling on the dark blue outfit we
saw in Infinity War, Marvel Studios experimented

00:02:58.000 --> 00:03:00.520
with a similar version of the look for Steve
Rogers.

00:03:01.520 --> 00:03:06.360
While most of the major players in Infinity
War were already introduced in previous films,

00:03:06.360 --> 00:03:11.360
the opening sequence brings a new team of
fresh faces to the fray: the Children of Thanos.

00:03:11.380 --> 00:03:16.260
Known in the pages of Marvel Comics as the
Black Order, this phalanx of cretins act as

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:17.520
heralds to the Mad Titan.

00:03:17.830 --> 00:03:21.980
They announce his arrival and carry out his
will on each unfortunate world that falls

00:03:21.980 --> 00:03:22.920
into his grasp.

00:03:23.020 --> 00:03:27.380
Since this quartet had never been on screen
before, Infinity War's design team had more

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.620
freedom than usual in their interpretation
of the characters.

00:03:30.870 --> 00:03:34.920
In particular, they experimented with how
expressive these frightening, gothic aliens

00:03:34.920 --> 00:03:35.600
should be.

00:03:35.740 --> 00:03:40.100
Some of the early passes at Thanos' children
find them a little more… smiley than the

00:03:40.100 --> 00:03:45.480
stern figures they became in the movie, particularly
these takes on Ebony Maw and Proxima Midnight.

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:50.720
It's an unwritten rule of the Marvel Cinematic
Universe that Tony Stark must don at least

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:53.990
one new suit of Iron Man armor every time
he appears.

00:03:53.990 --> 00:03:55.520
Infinity War is no exception.

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:00.200
The suits have been growing increasingly high-tech
ever since Iron Man 2's suitcase armor.

00:04:00.540 --> 00:04:05.580
But Infinity War's Bleeding Edge armor represents
a whole new frontier, using nanotechnology

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.270
to create a form-fitting, hyper-responsive
second skin that forms around Stark at his

00:04:10.270 --> 00:04:10.840
command.

00:04:10.960 --> 00:04:15.160
It's enough to make the first Iron Man movie's
technology look downright antiquated.

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:18.780
"Tony Stark was able to build this in a cave!"

00:04:19.180 --> 00:04:23.080
Marvel's design team put no small amount of
thought into the construction of the Bleeding

00:04:23.080 --> 00:04:24.080
Edge suit.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.170
As concept artist Phil Saunders explained,

00:04:26.170 --> 00:04:31.090
"[The suit is] actually forming all the anatomical
hairs underneath [...] There's a sort of neurological

00:04:31.090 --> 00:04:36.180
layer and a circulatory system layer that
gets formed, and then a layer of musculature."

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:39.920
It's probably for the best that the movie
doesn't linger on this in-between phase of

00:04:39.920 --> 00:04:45.040
the armor — it bears a disconcerting resemblance
to the off-putting, flayed muscle look of

00:04:45.040 --> 00:04:46.220
2011's Green Lantern.

00:04:47.360 --> 00:04:52.480
Comic book history is so rich that even costumes
can have long, complex legacies of their own.

00:04:52.480 --> 00:04:57.960
Such is the case with the Iron Spider, a variation
on Spider-Man's identity occasionally adopted

00:04:57.960 --> 00:04:59.040
by Peter Parker.

00:04:59.220 --> 00:05:03.840
The version of the suit deployed in Infinity
War is basically a somewhat shiny variant

00:05:03.840 --> 00:05:08.240
on the standard red-and-blue motif, but the
development team also tried a red-and-gold

00:05:08.240 --> 00:05:12.210
iteration that struck much more closely to
the comics that inspired it.

00:05:12.210 --> 00:05:16.320
There was also a black version, though that
one perhaps bore too much resemblance to the

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:19.060
symbiote suit seen in Spider-Man 3.

00:05:19.060 --> 00:05:20.530
Wouldn't want to remind anyone of that.

00:05:21.530 --> 00:05:26.140
In his quest for the Soul Stone, Thanos must
travel to the planet Vormir and confront the

00:05:26.140 --> 00:05:28.060
guardian of the gem that's hidden there.

00:05:28.060 --> 00:05:32.290
In a twist that shocked fans, this guardian
is revealed to be none other than the Red

00:05:32.290 --> 00:05:37.310
Skull, last seen getting banished to another
dimension by the Tesseract at the end of Captain

00:05:37.310 --> 00:05:38.920
America: The First Avenger.

00:05:39.040 --> 00:05:43.500
It goes without saying that the Skull has
been subjected to some intense cosmic punishment

00:05:43.500 --> 00:05:48.790
since we last saw him, and his decades of
isolation fired up the imaginations of Infinity

00:05:48.790 --> 00:05:49.940
War's character designers.

00:05:50.020 --> 00:05:53.280
Concept artist Ian Joyner explained his take,
saying,

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:57.360
"The idea of really hollowing him out and
embracing the more horrific aspects of his

00:05:57.360 --> 00:06:00.370
look was something I took a lot of delight
in exploring."

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:05.340
The team's takes on the new Red Skull run
the body horror gamut from a.. thing that

00:06:05.340 --> 00:06:09.510
wouldn't be out of place in a Guillermo del
Toro movie, to a haunting vision of a cloaked

00:06:09.510 --> 00:06:12.860
wraith that would be very much at home on
an Iron Maiden album cover.

00:06:13.000 --> 00:06:17.460
In the end, the look was probably too metal
for the movie but as with all of these prototype

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:19.690
designs, it's neat to know what could've been.

